  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nLatest RAP13 analysis depicts the upper low center nearing the Bay of \nFundy. This low will lift to the northeast through early this \nevening, allowing shortwave ridging to briefly build in along with \nwith somewhat drier air. This will bring an end to the lingering \nlight precipitation across western ME a little later this afternoon \nand some breaks in the clouds, especially into southern and SW ME. \nFor those that do see some thinning of the clouds, it will be a bit \nbreezy this afternoon with gusts up to around 30 mph.\n\nWinds diminish as the sun sets, and even if there is a period of \npartial clearing this evening, clouds likely fill back in tonight as \na 500mb trough swings through. This will bring more upslope \nprecipitation in the mountains with snow and more light \naccumulations possible in the higher terrain. Overnight lows will be \nin the 30s and 40s.\n\nThe 500mb pattern depicts troughing over the eastern half of Canada \nand into the Northeastern CONUS through the middle of this week. At \nthe surface, low pressure will be in the vicinity of the Canadian \nMaritimes, resulting a west to northwest flow. This setup will favor \nperiods of upslope precipitation and mostly cloudy skies in the \nmountains, which could be further enhanced at times as multiple \nshortwaves aloft send a couple of fronts through New England. \nDepending on the time of the waves, it's possible snow could mix in \nat times in the valleys but is more likely in the terrain, and the \nhigher peaks could pick up additional light snow accumulations.\n\nFor areas downwind of the mountains, there is lower potential for \nshowers, but with cyclonic flow/weak instability aloft, my suspicion \nis that there will probably be periods of mostly cloudy skies each \nday.\n\nAs far as temperatures go, highs will be mostly in the upper 40s to \nmid 50s south of the mountains Monday-Wednesday. Highs will be \nlimited to the 40s around the mountains with more cloud cover \nand potential for showers. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nA deep trough takes shape over the Great Lakes Region on \nThursday generating a surface low over the Ohio Valley. The \ntrough looks to cut off into a closed low and continues to push \neast along with the surface low Thursday night. On Friday the \nsurface low moves off the coast of the Mid Atlantic and is swung\nnorthward by the deepening closed upper low in it's wake, and \nthis is the point where model consensus ends. The track the low \ntakes up the coast is going to determine what impacts we see, if\nany at all. One of the more clear trends that has been present \nhas been for a more inland track with coastal areas seeing more \nrain. In fact, as I write this the 12Z deterministic GFS is \ncoming in with very little snow as it has been pushing for a \nmuch more amplified trough that directs the center of the low \ninland right though our area the last couple of runs. This has \nbeen an outlier as ensembles have a majority of low centers over\nthe ocean, but spread is also leaning westerly. \n\nAt this point I would say the higher peaks, and northern zones \nhave the best chance of seeing measurable snow, with the coast \nmore likely to see wind and rain if trends hold. Inland areas \nare anyone's guess as precipitation type there is extremely \nsensitive to track shifts and until we have a clearer consensus \non track it will remain low confidence. Global ensemble members \ncontinue to be all over the place with rain and snow amounts as \na result of this track uncertainty, but the overall trend the \nlast few runs has been an increase in number of members with \nmeasurable precipitation. We will continue to monitor trends, \nbut it is going to take more time before we can start breaking \ndown potential impacts.",
